### What are helpful NullPointerExceptions?

Helpful NullPointerExceptions (JEP 358, Java 14, on by default since Java 15) make the JVM describe exactly which variable or expression was null in an NPE message. Before this feature, a NullPointerException gave only a line number, which was useless for lines with multiple dereferences.

### What did NPE messages look like before?

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class StatementService {

    public String getHolderEmail(String accountId) {
        return repo.find(accountId).getHolder().getEmail();
        // Line 8: NullPointerException
    }
}
```

```text
Before JEP 358:
java.lang.NullPointerException
    at com.bank.service.StatementService.getHolderEmail(StatementService.java:8)
// Which was null? repo? find()? getHolder()? No idea.

After JEP 358:
java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke "Account.getHolder()"
because the return value of "AccountRepository.find(String)" is null
    at com.bank.service.StatementService.getHolderEmail(StatementService.java:8)
// Clear: repo.find() returned null.
```

### How does it work internally?

When an NPE occurs, the JVM re-walks the bytecode of the failing instruction to reconstruct a human-readable description of the null reference. This happens only when the exception is thrown, so there is no runtime cost during normal execution.

### Why is this diagnosis, not prevention?

The application still crashes. The customer still sees an error. The helpful message only makes it faster for the developer to identify the root cause.

```java
// The crash still happens:
try {
    String email = repo.find(accountId).getHolder().getEmail();
} catch (NullPointerException e) {
    // e.getMessage() now tells you WHICH part was null
    // But the code still threw an exception!
}
```

### How do you PREVENT NPEs?

Five approaches prevent NPEs instead of diagnosing them.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Optional;

public class SafeStatementService {

    // Approach 1: Optional return type
    public Optional<String> getHolderEmail(String accountId) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(repo.find(accountId))
            .map(Account::getHolder)
            .map(Holder::getEmail);
    }

    // Approach 2: Explicit null check
    public String getHolderEmailOrDefault(String accountId) {
        Account account = repo.find(accountId);
        if (account == null) return "no-email@bank.com";
        Holder holder = account.getHolder();
        if (holder == null) return "no-email@bank.com";
        return holder.getEmail() != null ? holder.getEmail() : "no-email@bank.com";
    }

    // Approach 3: Objects.requireNonNull at method entry
    public void processAccount(Account account) {
        java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(account, "Account must not be null");
        // Throws NPE immediately with a clear message if null
    }
}
```

### What is the difference between diagnosis and prevention?

| Aspect | Diagnosis (JEP 358) | Prevention (Optional/@NonNull) |
|---|---|---|
| Crash | Yes, still crashes | No crash, handled gracefully |
| Message | "X was null" | N/A (no exception thrown) |
| Runtime cost | None (only on crash) | Minimal (null check or Optional) |
| Code change | None (automatic) | Requires design changes |
| User impact | Error page | Graceful fallback |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: relying on helpful NPEs instead of null safety
public String getEmail(String id) {
    return repo.find(id).getHolder().getEmail();
    // "The message will tell us what's null" -- bad attitude
    // The customer still sees an error page!
}

// CORRECT: design the API to prevent nulls
public String getEmail(String id) {
    return repo.findById(id)                         // returns Optional<Account>
        .flatMap(account -> Optional.ofNullable(account.getHolder()))
        .map(Holder::getEmail)
        .orElse("unknown@bank.com");                 // graceful fallback
}
```

### How do helpful NPEs work with arrays?

```java
int[][] matrix = new int[3][];
matrix[0] = new int[]{1, 2, 3};
// matrix[1] is null
int value = matrix[1][0];
```

```text
NullPointerException: Cannot load from int array because "matrix[1]" is null
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Helpful NPEs were controlled by `-XX:+ShowCodeDetailsInExceptionMessages` in Java 14, but are on by default from Java 15.
- The description is computed lazily only when `getMessage()` is called, so there is no performance impact on throw.
- For auto-unboxing NPEs: `Integer x = null; int y = x;` now says `Cannot invoke "Integer.intValue()" because "x" is null`.
- In production logging, the improved message appears in stack traces, making log analysis faster.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does JEP 358 have a runtime cost? (No. The message is computed lazily on `getMessage()`, not on throw.)
- Can you disable helpful NPEs? (Yes, with `-XX:-ShowCodeDetailsInExceptionMessages`, but there is no reason to.)
- What is the best way to prevent NPEs? (Return `Optional` from methods that may not find a result. Use `@NonNull` annotations for parameters that must not be null.)
